{"status":"supported","evidence_ids":["ev-786888a5640f90b907bc2080","ev-7a8ba3a18e00ee715fea816e","ev-2009d89ce8c6ae63a7433535"],"request_dft":false,"proposed_test_ids":[],"rationale":"The claim is bound to the fixed protocol. Both relaxations are admissible converged_dft records with the same method_signature and protocol_id: Nb-bcc a = 3.3237 Å and Ta-bcc a = 3.3192 Å. Their difference is 0.0045 Å, which I checked by arithmetic and which matches the comparison record. That is below the 0.01 Å threshold by about a factor of 2.2. Residual stresses are small (0.423 and 0.112 kbar), so incomplete relaxation does not threaten the margin. No admissible records disagree.

Caveats: the protocol was not independently convergence-tested, so the margin rests on the plausible but unmeasured assumption that basis-set and k-mesh errors largely cancel between two isoelectronic bcc metals. Gemini-flash raised this point and claude-sonnet answered it. I find claude-sonnet's reply adequate: a differential error above about 0.0055 Å would be needed to flip the verdict. The 'consistent with size-matched for mixed-cation site ordering' clause is interpretive. Elemental bcc lattice constants are a weak proxy for oxide cation size matching. The decision criterion tests only the elemental difference, and I judge the claim on that criterion. Scope is 'not specified', so I read it as the claim's own 'under the fixed protocol' scope, which the records cover. If scope were read as requiring mixed-cation oxide coverage, the verdict would be unresolved.

The only offered test, relax-nb-and-ta-bcc-same-protocol, has already been done, so it fills no visible gap. A new DFT run is not needed to decide the claim."}
